==================================================================================================================
VERDICT
==================================================================================================================
  NO MERGE.  The CMC foliation and the unimodular clock are two structures, and they fit together rather than fuse:
  - the CMC multiplier is a LOCAL second-class constraint family whose global part is pure gauge (mu N -> mu N + c(t));
  - HT's local constraints are first class (gauge) and its content is ONE global pair (Lambda_0, T), which on the khronon's
    leaves can be written as a single global term; T labels the CMC leaves (a gauge choice), it defines none (Kuchar 1991);
  - every single-term merger fails: Lambda == f(K) freezes York time (H(z) = const), the flux form pins K = 1/l and lets Lambda
    drift, mu == beta sqrt(Lambda) loses the CMC condition and freezes the khronon, and one global squared constraint is irregular.
  Combined (not merged): FP14's local count + one global pair; FRW growth, the static coupling and the York/CMC evasion unchanged.
  The a0 tie stays XR20's T1: a0 reads the conserved Lambda_0 = K_inf^2/3; K_inf adds nothing new, and the local York time gives
  the rival a0 ~ H(z).  The literature's 'unimodular shape dynamics' (Gryb-Thebault 2012) is the same pairing.
  kappa = 1/2 stays FITTED; nothing here derives it.  The closure target stays open.

